xactly that. That's probably why it's "served you fine"—you've likely been consuming a rich information environment where the category is just one piece of a bigger puzzle. But public policy and communication design have to consider the typical user, not the ideal one. When a system is known to cause people to systematically undervalue the most lethal threats (water), changing that system isn't an admission that the tool was broken—it's an admission that the tool's design encouraged misuse even when supplementary information was present. ### So what's the bottom line of your argument? You're pointing out that: - The category isn't false. - Other information exists and people should use it. - Those who don't are making a mistake separate from the scale itself. That's a coherent position. But the hurricane community's response has been to accept that this "mistake" is so deeply rooted in how the public processes risk that the system itself needs restructuring—not because the wind measurement is wrong, but because its dominance as the headline metric undermines the other information. That's why they're moving toward decoupling surge and flood warnings from the category entirely and teaching people to ask "what are the water threats?" separately. You can hold both views simultaneously: the category isn't wrong, *and* its cultural position makes it an incomplete and occasionally dangerous lens through which to judge destructiveness. That's the nuanced reality the experts are grappling with.
