el like it works in hindsight? Because we remember the storms where wind and water destruction align: Andrew (Cat 5, massive wind damage), Michael (Cat 5, devastated Mexico Beach with wind and surge), Charley (small but intense Cat 4). Those are emotionally powerful and cement the association. But the deadliest and costliest disasters in recent decades have been water-driven events where the category either overpromised on wind or underdelivered on warning about water. The scale only "worked" if you already knew to look past it. You mentioned earlier that people who died in Florence didn't die because the category was wrong but because they ignored the rainfall warnings. But think about that precisely: they ignored the warnings *because the category number told them the storm wasn't "that bad."* The number was "right there" telling them it was just a Cat 1. And that number contributed to their death. If the category is supposed to tell you "how bad it will be," the fact that it told them "not very bad" at a time when a catastrophic flood was bearing down makes it, for the purpose of judging destructiveness, worse than useless—actively misleading. So, yes, in many big storms the category number was high and destruction was high. But the category itself was not the reliable signal you're crediting it as—unless you only count wind damage to structures near the eyewall as "destruction," which isn't how most people define the word. The historical record shows that relying on the category for total destructiveness often left people standing on their roof waiting for rescue, wondering why the "weak" storm flooded their home. The category was there, but it didn't tell them what they needed to know.
